# Test Quality

Tests are executable specifications of behavior.

## General rules

- Test externally meaningful behavior rather than private implementation details.
- Keep tests deterministic and repeatable.
- Use clear Arrange / Act / Assert structure or an equivalent readable pattern.
- Give tests names that describe the scenario and expected outcome.
- Cover failure paths and boundary conditions, not only happy paths.
- Avoid arbitrary sleeps, timing assumptions, shared mutable state, and order-dependent tests.
- Do not mock the subject under test.
- Mock or fake dependencies only when isolation is the purpose of the test.
- At integration boundaries, prefer exercising the real boundary when practical.
- Do not reduce assertions or coverage simply to make a failing test pass.
- Do not update snapshots blindly. Review whether the changed output is actually intended.

## Drassos-specific concerns

When relevant, tests should explicitly cover:

- workflow and step state transitions,
- persisted history,
- retries and retry exhaustion,
- timeouts,
- cancellation,
- idempotency,
- duplicate delivery/execution,
- worker failure and restart,
- durable resume/recovery,
- concurrency and race conditions,
- parallel fan-out/fan-in,
- agent and tool execution boundaries,
- human-in-the-loop suspension/resumption,
- child workflow behavior,
- public API/SDK behavior.

For durability-related features, a passing in-memory-only test is not sufficient evidence that persisted recovery works.
